From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Davi <davilimacarv2536@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:07:49 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] collection writes only what changes

A collection rewrites a lot of state with the value it already holds:
the epilogue clears caches that are already empty, every profile of
every CodeBlock it visits is merged into itself, the allocator's
bookkeeping is reset to where it stands, and an atomic test-and-set
writes a mark bit that is already set. In a process forked from a
parent that prepared the heap, every such store copies a page it shared
with the parent. On the Oracle A1 a worker's first full collection made
0.4 to 0.9 MiB of the zygote's pages private this way, most of them
holding the bytes they held before.

Each of those sites now stores only a value that differs: the VM caches
reset only entries that are not empty, the profiles store only a merged
prediction or mode that changed, the directory's bit vectors store only
the words that change, the free lists, allocator cursors, weak block
links and JIT stub flags skip a store of the same value, the
megamorphic cache leaves invalid entries alone, and a block's mark bit
is tested with a plain load before the atomic test-and-set.

Cell colors are left as they were. Keeping a cell that is still black
from the last cycle black until its visit would save more pages, but
the collector's grey and black stores are what orders its mark against
the mutator's whitening barrier, and without them a marked, scanned
cell can end white on a weakly ordered CPU and lose its barrier.
